<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Augustus Freeman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1823">1823</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Augustus Freeman was an English historian and Regius Professor of Modern History at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> from 1884. His best-known work is the History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and in his History of Federal Government he made the Achaean League a model of a federal state. Admired for his learning, he is now also criticised for his racial views of the English past.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-augustus-freeman.jpg" alt="Edward Augustus Freeman" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elliott &amp; Fry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Augustus_Freeman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Freeman was born on 2 August 1823 at Metchley Abbey in Harborne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwickshire">Warwickshire</a>, and his parents named him after the month of his birth. Both died in November 1824, and he was raised by his paternal grandmother at Weston-super-Mare. He won a scholarship to Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, became a fellow of the college in 1845 and, though drawn to the High Church movement, gave up the idea of taking orders. In 1847 he married Eleanor Gutch, daughter of his former tutor, and in 1860 settled at Somerleaze, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His first book was a History of Architecture (1849), and his essay on window tracery of 1851 proposed the terms Flowing and Flamboyant. He published 239 distinct works, among them William Rufus (1882) and the unfinished History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, on whose fourth volume he worked with his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a>. He insisted on the unity of history and on the right use of original authorities, though he did not use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and he often said that &quot;History is past politics and politics present history.&quot; The saying marks the limit of his work, which was almost wholly political history and dealt little with other sides of a nation's life.</p><p>A follower of Gladstone, he stood unsuccessfully for Mid-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> in 1868 and took a prominent part in the agitation over the Bulgarian atrocities of the April Uprising. He expressed an antipathy for the Turks and sympathy for the subject nations of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and he and Evans were activists in the uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. His private letters show disdain for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>: he called Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli">Benjamin Disraeli</a> &quot;the dirty Jew&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1886 ill health kept him abroad for much of the time. On a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1892 he fell ill with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a> on 16 March, where he was buried in the Protestant cemetery under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscription by Evans. His library of some 6,500 volumes went to Owens College, Manchester, and most of his papers, including 6,200 architectural sketches of European churches, are in the University of Manchester Library.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Augustus Freeman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Victorian historian <b>Edward Augustus Freeman</b> made it the centrepiece of his History of Federal Government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Harborne, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.45,-1.97&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Harborne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4506 354 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward Augustus Freeman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4956 624h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4956 624h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.45,-1.97&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
